\section*{Amenability of Thompson Group and Interval Homeomorphism Groups}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a counterexample in the selected manuscript. Thompson group $F$ is claimed to be nonamenable.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For Thompson's group \(F\), consisting of increasing piecewise-linear homeomorphisms of \([0,1]\) with dyadic breakpoints and slopes powers of two, the source claims
\[
 F\text{ is nonamenable}.
\]
This is a statement about the standard discrete group \(F\).

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Recursive colors on dyadic partitions are designed to behave under affine transport. Finite averages of these colors give a uniform boundary discrepancy under a fixed set of group moves, contradicting the existence of Følner sets.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Theorem 1.1 proves F nonamenable. Since F is a subgroup of PL\_+(I), amenability passing to subgroups proves PL\_+(I) nonamenable as well; both questions receive negative answers. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed negative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 248, the documented statement rules out a positive normalized invariant mean for standard dyadic Thompson $F$; it does not specify an explicit expansion constant. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ThompsonNonamenability.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.ThompsonNonamenability.thompson\_F\_nonamenable\_composition}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

